      "level": 1,
      "content": "Example: Lambda Capturing Variables"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "content": "1#include <iostream>\n2\n3using namespace std;\n4\n5int main(){\n6    int i = 5;\n7    auto val = [=](int x, int y) -> int {\n8        return i + x + y;\n9    } (5,5);\n10\n11\n12    cout << \"The sum is : \" << val;\n13}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Output:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "content": "The sum is : 15"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here, note the [=] expression, which indicates that we are capturing the variables in surrounding scope by value."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "This [=] expression allows us to use variables in the surrounding scope within the lambda body."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "We can then reference the variable i within the lambda body."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Idea Emoji",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "We can also capture the surrounding variables by reference using [&], which we'll later."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
